TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit; Appendix A: STK files and settings"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Images 1, 3, 4 and 5; Appendix A: STK files and settings"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary; Contents; Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Table 3","issue":"The estimates 2,698 km / 560 km ≈ 5 and 2,698 km / 60 km ≈ 45 are dimensionless ratios, not days. They use adjacent-track spacing without establishing how the ground-track pattern shifts or repeats over time. The stated five-day access and 45-day mosaic are therefore unsupported; the STK figures show ground tracks, not sensor access or coverage of the reef."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"A 50 Mbit/s downlink capacity is assumed without a link budget. Antenna gains, transmit power, frequency allocation, modulation/coding, losses and link margin are not established, so the claimed daily capacity margin is unverified."},{"where":"2.1 Spacecraft overview","issue":"The 0.10 m aperture is compared only with an ideal diffraction limit at 0.5 µm. That calculation alone does not establish 5 m multispectral image resolution or GSD; focal length, detector sampling, optical performance and signal-to-noise are not demonstrated."},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The drag-sail lifetime estimate uses a simplified constant-density, scale-height model. The report acknowledges large solar-cycle variation, but does not provide a bounded lifetime analysis that verifies disposal within 25 years across environmental uncertainty."}],"overall":"A strong, well-structured mission report with a clear Australian benefit, substantial design work and useful STK evidence. The principal weakness is the unsupported, dimensionally incorrect revisit and mosaic timing; the payload-performance and downlink assumptions also need verification.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Clearly explains the mission need, direct bleaching-mapping concept, rationale for using one spacecraft, and named operator and customers. The discussion is detailed and supported by relevant citations; a few cited sources are less authoritative than primary scientific literature.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"One satellite is therefore used."},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS)"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":42.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Addresses launch choice, orbit and elements, manoeuvres, ground-station accesses, data volume, mission duration and disposal, with STK views, access data and hand calculations. The coverage-time derivation is dimensionally invalid and unsupported by a target-access simulation; the link budget is also absent. These significant limitations prevent a higher mark.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The baseline is an SSMS rideshare flight on Vega C from Kourou in early 2025"},{"where":"Table 3","quote":"Days to reach any point of the reef | 2,698 km / 560 km | about 5 days"},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"A full link budget for the X-band downlink has not been calculated in this report"},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings","quote":"The following STK 11 files are submitted with this report in ZYZZsat_STK.zip."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion draws specific outcomes from the design, recaps performance and EOL estimates, and openly identifies limitations and next steps. Some results it relies on, especially revisit and mosaic timing, are not securely established by the calculations.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations."},{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"With these points addressed in the next design phase, the mission can provide the direct, regular bleaching maps that current temperature-based monitoring cannot."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Uses a clear engineering-report structure, concise executive summary, numbered sections, labelled tables and figures, and an identifiable bibliography with in-text citations. The writing is generally readable, though occasionally awkward; some sources are incomplete or less authoritative, and the unsupported coverage claims weaken the presentation of results.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The design is supported by STK simulations and by hand calculations of the orbit, coverage, data budget and orbital decay."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"Figure 1: STK 3D view of the ZYZZsat orbit at two times 12 hours apart (generated in STK 11)."},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[11] D. A. Vallado, Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications, 4th ed."}]}]}
